      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys-quadripunctatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>A cute dwarf livebearer from Costa Rica, only very rarely on offer. Males grow to about 1.5 cm, females to a maximum of twice this size. The species name refers to the typically four spots on the flanks, but many variations occur in nature: some specimens have only one spot, others five, though most show the pattern of the females photographed for this post.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys-quadripunctatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>In nature, these dwarfs are exposed to extremely different water conditions. In the original scientific description, Bussing describes several biotopes, including a partly shaded, partly sunlit forest stream with clear, 20°C cool water, and turbid, fully sun-exposed residual water puddles where the water was 34°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/phallichthys-quadripunctatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Bussing also mentions that this species is shy in the aquarium, but loses its shyness when kept with other, less shy fish in a densely planted aquarium.</p>"
    }
  ],
